WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.408
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.708 --> 00:00:08.886
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.186 --> 00:00:17.887
Unit thirty-three: Humour, Irony and Satire. In this unit, you will analyse how humour criticises and connects.

4
00:00:18.883 --> 00:00:20.117
sarcasm.

5
00:00:20.817 --> 00:00:26.485
sarcasm means saying the opposite of what you mean in order to mock or criticise.

6
00:00:26.785 --> 00:00:30.735
'Great job,' he said with heavy sarcasm after I dropped the plates.

7
00:00:31.435 --> 00:00:34.169
Your turn. Say: sarcasm.

8
00:00:36.869 --> 00:00:37.974
sarcasm.

9
00:00:38.983 --> 00:00:40.112
satirical.

10
00:00:40.812 --> 00:00:45.586
satirical means using humour and exaggeration to criticise people or society.

11
00:00:45.886 --> 00:00:50.765
The satirical cartoon showed the minister asleep on a pile of promises.

12
00:00:51.465 --> 00:00:53.885
Your turn. Say: satirical.

13
00:00:56.585 --> 00:00:57.528
satirical.

14
00:00:58.517 --> 00:00:59.402
parody.

15
00:01:00.102 --> 00:01:05.491
parody means a humorous imitation of a well-known work or style.

16
00:01:05.791 --> 00:01:09.172
Students performed a parody of a TV news programme.

17
00:01:09.872 --> 00:01:12.873
Your turn. Say: parody.

18
00:01:15.573 --> 00:01:16.400
parody.

19
00:01:17.417 --> 00:01:18.186
wit.

20
00:01:18.886 --> 00:01:23.497
wit means the ability to say clever and amusing things quickly.

21
00:01:23.797 --> 00:01:26.598
Her wit made even the dullest meeting enjoyable.

22
00:01:27.298 --> 00:01:30.113
Your turn. Say: wit.

23
00:01:32.813 --> 00:01:33.454
wit.

24
00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:35.439
dark humour.

25
00:01:36.139 --> 00:01:40.728
dark humour means jokes about serious, painful or frightening subjects.

26
00:01:41.028 --> 00:01:44.247
Doctors sometimes use dark humour to cope with stress.

27
00:01:44.947 --> 00:01:47.982
Your turn. Say: dark humour.

28
00:01:50.682 --> 00:01:51.672
dark humour.

29
00:01:52.683 --> 00:01:54.126
self-deprecation.

30
00:01:54.826 --> 00:01:58.358
self-deprecation means making modest jokes about yourself.

31
00:01:58.658 --> 00:02:02.016
A little self-deprecation makes a speaker more likeable.

32
00:02:02.716 --> 00:02:05.728
Your turn. Say: self-deprecation.

33
00:02:08.428 --> 00:02:09.870
self-deprecation.

34
00:02:10.883 --> 00:02:11.803
deadpan.

35
00:02:12.503 --> 00:02:17.545
deadpan means delivered with a completely serious face, though meant to be funny.

36
00:02:17.845 --> 00:02:20.692
His deadpan delivery made the joke even funnier.

37
00:02:21.392 --> 00:02:24.044
Your turn. Say: deadpan.

38
00:02:26.744 --> 00:02:27.675
deadpan.

39
00:02:28.683 --> 00:02:29.975
incongruity.

40
00:02:30.675 --> 00:02:36.587
incongruity means something that does not fit with what is expected, often a source of humour.

41
00:02:36.887 --> 00:02:41.545
Much comedy comes from incongruity: a king slipping on a banana.

42
00:02:42.245 --> 00:02:45.489
Your turn. Say: incongruity.

43
00:02:48.189 --> 00:02:49.690
incongruity.

44
00:02:50.683 --> 00:02:53.020
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:57.618
Ms. Sarah, why do some jokes work in English but not in Khmer?

46
00:02:58.917 --> 00:03:05.551
Humour depends on shared expectations. Break them, and people laugh; translate them, and often they don't.

47
00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:09.944
Like sarcasm? Some of my friends never use it.

48
00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:17.153
Some don't, and some do all the time. Sarcasm, in particular, travels badly.

49
00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:21.311
What about satire? Is it just mockery?

50
00:03:22.617 --> 00:03:29.042
Not just mockery. Satirical writers criticise power; mockery merely laughs at people.

51
00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:34.140
And deadpan comedy? That, I find hard to read.

52
00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:39.772
Hard to read it may be, but master it and you'll understand British humour.

53
00:03:41.083 --> 00:03:46.717
Grammar focus: Ellipsis, substitution and fronting (extended).

54
00:03:47.017 --> 00:03:50.920
Ellipsis and fronting make speech efficient and emphatic.

55
00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:58.482
Some don't use sarcasm, and some do all the time. Deadpan comedy, that I find hard to read.

56
00:03:58.782 --> 00:04:02.558
Hard to read it may be, but master it and you'll understand.

57
00:04:02.858 --> 00:04:10.813
Ellipsis avoids repetition; fronting moves the key idea to the start for emphasis or contrast.

58
00:04:11.817 --> 00:04:14.295
Now read and listen. Why We Laugh.

59
00:04:14.595 --> 00:04:17.871
Philosophers have long tried to explain why we laugh.

60
00:04:19.183 --> 00:04:26.860
One influential theory holds that humour arises from incongruity, the gap between what we expect and what happens.

61
00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:34.480
Wit plays with this gap through clever language, while parody exaggerates the style of something familiar.

62
00:04:35.783 --> 00:04:43.367
Satirical writing, from Jonathan Swift to modern cartoonists, uses humour to expose the abuse of power.

63
00:04:44.683 --> 00:04:50.503
Sarcasm can be playful, but it can also wound, and it often fails across cultures.

64
00:04:51.817 --> 00:04:56.069
Dark humour helps people cope with tragedy, though it can easily offend.

65
00:04:57.383 --> 00:05:03.481
Self-deprecation, by contrast, invites listeners to laugh with the speaker rather than at others.

66
00:05:04.783 --> 00:05:13.099
Humour, in short, is never merely funny: it reveals what a society values, fears and refuses to say aloud.

67
00:05:14.383 --> 00:05:17.279
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:17.579 --> 00:05:21.030
A humorous imitation of a well-known work is a blank.

69
00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:25.715
A, parody. B, sarcasm. C, deadpan.

70
00:05:29.717 --> 00:05:32.076
The answer is A: parody.

71
00:05:33.583 --> 00:05:38.741
In 'Some students like satire; others don't', what is left out after 'don't'?

72
00:05:39.041 --> 00:05:43.774
A, like satire. B, students. C, others.

73
00:05:47.783 --> 00:05:50.491
The answer is A: like satire.

74
00:05:51.983 --> 00:05:55.179
'That, I find hard to read' is an example of blank.

75
00:05:55.479 --> 00:06:00.607
A, fronting. B, passive voice. C, a conditional.

76
00:06:04.617 --> 00:06:06.988
The answer is A: fronting.

77
00:06:08.483 --> 00:06:11.342
Making modest jokes about yourself is blank.

78
00:06:11.642 --> 00:06:16.677
A, self-deprecation. B, dark humour. C, incongruity.

79
00:06:20.683 --> 00:06:23.449
The answer is A: self-deprecation.

80
00:06:24.950 --> 00:06:28.900
According to the text, what does satirical writing expose?

81
00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:35.129
A, the abuse of power. B, grammar mistakes. C, personal secrets.

82
00:06:39.117 --> 00:06:41.755
The answer is A: the abuse of power.

83
00:06:43.250 --> 00:06:48.398
Now it's your turn. Part one: Should satire about political leaders be protected?

84
00:06:53.698 --> 00:06:59.878
Part two: Use one example of ellipsis and one of fronting; give two reasons.

85
00:07:06.178 --> 00:07:18.295
Satire, I strongly believe, deserves protection. Powerful people can defend themselves; ordinary citizens often can't. Uncomfortable it may be, but satire keeps leaders humble.

86
00:07:18.595 --> 00:07:21.221
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:22.217 --> 00:07:28.851
Great work today! You learned eight new words and ellipsis, substitution and fronting (extended).

88
00:07:29.151 --> 00:07:33.011
Today's value is laughter. Laughing together makes us happy.

89
00:07:33.311 --> 00:07:36.091
See you in Unit thirty-four. Goodbye!

90
00:07:36.391 --> 00:07:39.067
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

